**Theorem 7.3 (all-short-group Farkas alternative).** Fix
$r\in[r_-,r_+]$ and an integer $m\ge0$. For each
$Q\subseteq\mathscr G$ with $|Q|\le m$, retain **all** endpoint
columns of its paired and singleton groups and put

$$
\widehat A_Q=\begin{pmatrix}A_{I_Q}\\\kappa_{I_Q}^T\end{pmatrix},
\qquad \widehat B_r=\begin{pmatrix}B\\r\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{FC.22}
$$

Then $n_t(r)>m$ if and only if, for every such $Q$, there exists
$z_Q\in\mathbb R^9$ with

$$
z_Q\cdot(\Gamma(x_i),k(x_i))\ge0\quad(i\in I_Q),\qquad
z_Q\cdot(B,r)<0.
\tag{FC.23}
$$

The vectors may depend on $Q$ and $r$. For $m=7$ or $8$ these
are finite all-alternative-optima exclusions. Testing only supports
of a selected optimizer, only one endpoint from a paired group,
or only strictly signed branches is insufficient.

Proof. The finite-dimensional Farkas alternative for
$\widehat A_Q w=\widehat B_r$, $w\ge0$, says that feasibility
is equivalent to the absence of (FC.23). One direction is immediate
by taking its dot product with a feasible nonnegative combination.
For the other, the cone generated by finitely many columns is
closed and convex, and separation of a point outside it gives a
functional nonnegative on that cone and negative at the point.
Thus (FC.23) is equivalent to emptiness of the slice of
$\mathcal P_Q$. By Theorem 5.2, emptiness for every selection
with at most $m$ groups is precisely $r\notin U_m$, or
$n_t(r)>m$. An empty selection also has its Farkas vector, since
$B$ has mass one. Compatible merging and the activity theorem
make this a test of every original short optimum, including
fractional ones. $\square$

